{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "iterative",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 10, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 8, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.70,
      "why": "Area is the sole objective and throughput only needs >=1 MSPS, which the shared-datapath iterative core meets easily (Fmax/(N+3) is still several MSPS for N<=18). Accuracy is family-independent, so for any accuracy point the iterative core is the smallest-area realisation and should own most of the Pareto front. Ranges bracket the accuracy constraint: W=10..20 spans output LSB 2^-8..2^-18 around the 2^-10 limit, N=8..18 spans the CORDIC angle-approximation floor, and the guard/rounding knobs let the search trade datapath bits for error."
    },
    {
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 10, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 8, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "k", "low": 2, "high": 4, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.15,
      "why": "Same accuracy model as iterative but k micro-rotations/cycle, so it should be area-dominated by iterative for this spec. A small share confirms whether any k=2 point sneaks onto the front (it cannot help throughput, which is already satisfied) and guards against a modelling surprise."
    },
    {
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 10, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 8, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "m", "low": 2, "high": 8, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.15,
      "why": "Registers every m stages: 1 result/cycle but N-stage datapath, so FFs scale with N and it should be area-dominated for a 1 MSPS target. Included at low share to verify it stays off the front and to catch any case where its higher Fmax changes the area/accuracy picture."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec is area-first: throughput >=1 MSPS is trivially met by every family (even iterative at N=18 gives several MSPS), and accuracy depends only on data_width/n_iter/angle_guard/frac_guard/rounding, not on the family. Therefore the Pareto front in (luts_plus_ffs, accuracy_bits) is almost certainly owned by the smallest-area family, iterative, and the other families are only worth probing to confirm domination. Round 1 is a broad sweep: W=10..20 straddles the 2^-10 error constraint (output LSB 2^-(W-2)), N=8..18 straddles the CORDIC angle-approximation floor, and the guard/rounding knobs expose the cheap accuracy levers. 70% of the budget goes to iterative to densely map the front near the constraint boundary and just above it (the HV reference is 1500 LUTs+FFs / 10 accuracy bits, so designs far above 1500 area or far below 10 bits are not worth many samples). The remaining 30% is split between unrolled_k and pipelined_m to test whether any non-iterative point is non-dominated; pipelined is omitted from round 1 because it is the largest-area family (N register stages) and cannot beat iterative on the only objective. Later rounds will re-allocate toward whichever family actually appears on the front and extend its ranges."
}

{
  "decision": "map_front",
  "next_plan": null,
  "rationale": "Round 3 of 4 is done (300/400 evals); only one 100-eval round remains, which is exactly the reserved budget for final front mapping. The feasible front already spans luts_plus_ffs 259..705 against an HV reference of 1500, and accuracy_bits 10..25.5 against a reference of 10, with the min-area corner (iterative W=14 N=14, 259 luts+ffs, 10.05 bits, 11.7 MSPS) satisfying both constraints (throughput >= 1, err <= 2^-10). The only unexplored family, pipelined, is 1 result/cycle and therefore the most area-heavy; with area as the sole objective and the accuracy ceiling already near the 28-bit/30-iteration limit (best seen 25.52 bits), it is very unlikely to place a non-dominated point on a min-area front, so spending the last round exploring it would risk leaving the existing front under-mapped. Mapping the full ranges of the families already on the front (iterative, unrolled_k, pipelined_m) is the best use of the final 100 evals to fill any gaps in the 259..705 / 10..25.5 region and confirm the front before selection."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "Final round and the full 400-eval budget is spent (400/400, 4/4 rounds). The front is feasible and well covered, so `infeasible` is not warranted: 0% of evaluations violate throughput (best seen 171 MSPS vs the >=1 MSPS constraint) and the error constraint is met with large margin (best 2.08e-08 = 2^-25.52 vs <=2^-10). The Pareto front spans luts_plus_ffs 259..705 against the HV reference 1500 and accuracy_bits 10..25.5 against the HV reference 10, i.e. it already covers the whole useful trade-off range, and the selection rule (min luts_plus_ffs) lands on iterative [data_width=14 n_iter=14 angle_guard=1 frac_guard=2 trunc] at 259 LUT+FF, 10.05 accuracy bits, 11.7 MSPS — comfortably inside both constraints. The last round added only +1.4% hypervolume, indicating the front has converged. The one unexplored family, pipelined, is a 1-result/cycle architecture whose nearest explored relative pipelined_m has best feasible luts_plus_ffs=884, ~3.4x the current area-optimal corner, so it cannot plausibly improve the min-area selection; with no budget left to map it, stopping is the correct call."
  ,"next_plan": null
}
